<h2 id="libro-fm-ebooks-announcement">Libro.fm Is Adding Ebooks — What We Know So Far</h2>

<p>On <strong>August 7, 2026</strong>, <a href="https://libro.fm/">Libro.fm</a> — the audiobook platform built around independent bookstores — announced that it will add <strong>ebooks</strong> to its catalog. The news was covered the same day by Publishers Weekly, Publishing Perspectives, and <a href="https://bookriot.com/ebooks-are-coming-to-libro-fm/">Book Riot</a>. Ebooks will be offered right alongside audiobooks inside the Libro.fm app, together with a set of new features designed for people who both read and listen.</p>

<p>For anyone who follows the ebook ecosystem, this is a meaningful development. Libro.fm has spent more than a decade building an audiobook store where every purchase supports an independent bookstore of your choosing. Extending that model to ebooks gives the indie bookstore movement a second credible ebook storefront — after Bookshop.org launched ebooks in January 2025 — and it gives readers a genuine alternative to buying digital books from Amazon, Kobo, or Google.</p>

<p>This guide covers what was announced, what the new features actually do, how the indie storefront trend is shaping up, and what it all means for EPUB readers. If you have been looking for a way to buy ebooks without feeding the Amazon ecosystem, the practical details below are worth your time.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="what-is-libro-fm">What Libro.fm Is and How the Indie Model Works</h2>

<p>Libro.fm launched in <strong>2014</strong> as an employee-owned Social Purpose Corporation, and it later became a certified B Corporation. Its stated mission is twofold: help people read more, and help independent bookstores sell more audiobooks. Today the company is partnered with more than <strong>4,000 bookstores around the world</strong>, with members in <strong>95 countries</strong>.</p>

<p>The core mechanic is simple and deliberately different from Audible or Amazon. When you buy an audiobook on Libro.fm, you choose the independent bookstore that gets credit for the sale, and that store earns a share of the revenue. The bookstore is not a drop-shipper; it is a real partner in the transaction. That arrangement is why bookstores promote Libro.fm to their customers and why the platform has grown steadily while Amazon's audiobook dominance has remained unchallenged in most other respects.</p>

<p>Lee Hooyboer, Libro.fm's Senior Manager of Bookseller Partnerships, framed the ebook expansion in those terms when the announcement was made: <em>"Especially over the past few years, we've seen how readers want to divest from Amazon. They want to support their indie because they recognize how important these third places are for their community. And now they can support them all the more with ebooks on Libro. These dollars will make a huge difference."</em></p>

<p>That quote captures the trend driving this launch. Amazon controls the vast majority of the ebook market, and independent bookstores receive essentially nothing when their customers buy Kindle editions. Platforms like Libro.fm and Bookshop.org exist to reroute some of that spending back to local stores — and ebooks are the largest digital book category those stores have been locked out of.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="new-features">The New Features: LibroSync, Flexible Credits, and More</h2>

<p>The ebook announcement came with four concrete features, all confirmed in Libro.fm's August 7 statement:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>LibroSync:</strong> a new capability for people who own both the audiobook and the ebook of a title. LibroSync lets you switch seamlessly between the two, and even follow along with the ebook text while the audiobook plays. It is the Libro.fm equivalent of Amazon's Whispersync for Voice — the "immersion reading" feature that lets Kindle and Audible users move between listening and reading without losing their place.</li>
  <li><strong>Flexible credits:</strong> Libro.fm's subscription credits will apply to <em>ebooks and audiobooks alike</em>. Previously credits were an audiobook-only currency; the change removes the friction of maintaining a separate ebook budget.</li>
  <li><strong>Member benefits:</strong> subscribers will get exclusive ebook offers alongside their existing audiobook discounts and perks.</li>
  <li><strong>Ebook Advance Reading Copies:</strong> members of Libro.fm's bookseller and influencer program will receive advance ebook copies in addition to the advance audiobook copies they already get — a meaningful tool for store staff who recommend titles to customers.</li>
</ul>

<p>LibroSync deserves the most attention, because it speaks directly to how people actually consume books in 2026. Hybrid reading — listening during a commute, then picking up the text at home — is one of the most popular ways to get through a book, and Amazon has long used Whispersync as a lock-in feature. A credible independent alternative matters for readers who want that experience without tying their whole library to Amazon.</p>

<p>The flexible-credits change is the quiet unlock. A monthly credit that can buy either format means you no longer have to decide in advance whether you are an "audiobook person" or an "ebook person." For listeners who occasionally want the text edition of a title they love — for highlighting, for reference, for reading aloud to a child — that flexibility is a real upgrade over the current split economy of separate audiobook and ebook subscriptions.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="indie-storefront-trend">The Indie Ebook Storefront Trend: Bookshop.org, Kobo, and Libro.fm</h2>

<p>Libro.fm is not the first independent platform to try this. <strong>Bookshop.org</strong> launched ebooks in <strong>January 2025</strong>, an event covered by WIRED, The Verge, The New York Times, and Engadget. Bookshop.org sells ebooks through its iOS and Android app, shares revenue with the bookstore of your choice, and has grown quickly as a home for readers who want to support local stores. It is the direct predecessor of what Libro.fm is now announcing.</p>

<p>Bookshop.org's path also shows how difficult ebook infrastructure is to build. A partnership that would let customers read Bookshop.org ebooks on <strong>Kobo e-readers</strong> was originally promised for 2025, then pushed to 2026, and at one point looked as though it might be delayed indefinitely. In July 2026, <a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/06/amazon-competitor-bookshop-org-says-kobo-e-reader-support-will-happen-this-year-after-all/">TechCrunch reported</a> that founder and CEO Andy Hunter said progress had finally been made and that Kobo support should roll out <strong>"later this year."</strong> Hunter explained the holdup in terms every ebook seller will recognize: the integration is something both Kobo and Bookshop.org want, but it required settling business terms and allocating engineering resources "in such a way that respects publisher requirements for digital rights management."</p>

<p>Libro.fm entering ebooks is another bet on the same thesis: readers will buy from indie storefronts if the experience is good enough. The two platforms may compete for the same customers — or they may converge on the same infrastructure, since both are app-first and both need the same industry plumbing (EPUB supply, DRM handling, e-reader integrations). Either way, the direction of travel is clear: the "buy ebooks only from Amazon" era is ending, and independent storefronts are racing to fill the gap.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="ebook-format">What Format Will Libro.fm Ebooks Use?</h2>

<p>Libro.fm has not yet specified the file format for its ebooks, so treat the details below as context rather than announcement. The key fact for readers: <strong>EPUB is the open standard used by every major non-Amazon storefront.</strong> Bookshop.org sells EPUBs, Kobo sells EPUBs, Google Play Books sells EPUBs, and most public library systems lend EPUBs. If you buy an ebook from a store that is not Amazon, you are almost certainly getting an EPUB file — the same container format that powers the <a href="/blog/epub-3-4-spec-2026/">EPUB 3.x specification</a> that this site converts documents into.</p>

<p>DRM is the other open question. Bookshop.org's Andy Hunter explicitly cited "publisher requirements for digital rights management" as part of the reason its Kobo integration took so long. The de facto protection layer for EPUBs sold outside Amazon is <strong>Adobe DRM</strong>, which is how Kobo, Bookshop.org, and most library systems protect files. It is reasonable to expect Libro.fm to make similar arrangements with publishers, though nothing has been announced yet.</p>

<p>What this means practically: an EPUB bought from a storefront like this will generally be readable in its own app and on EPUB-native e-readers such as Kobo, PocketBook, and Boox. If the file is DRM-free, you can manage it freely in Calibre alongside the rest of your library — the pattern we cover in our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library guide</a>. The structural difference from Amazon is that your files are not locked to one brand of hardware; EPUB is designed to move between devices.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="reading-on-your-device">How to Read Indie Storefront Ebooks on Your Device</h2>

<p>If you are planning to buy ebooks from Libro.fm or Bookshop.org when they become available, here is what to expect on each type of device:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Phone and tablet (immediate):</strong> both indie storefronts start with their own apps for iOS and Android. Reading in the app works the day ebooks launch, and app reading is where LibroSync-style features live.</li>
  <li><strong>EPUB-native e-readers:</strong> Kobo, PocketBook, Boox, and Meebook devices read EPUB files directly, so a DRM-free EPUB from an indie storefront can be side-loaded and read in minutes. Our <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook comparison</a> walks through the differences between the major EPUB-native families.</li>
  <li><strong>Kindle:</strong> EPUB is not natively supported on most Kindle hardware, so Kindle owners need to convert EPUB files and side-load them. The standard tool is Calibre, and our <a href="/blog/calibre-epub-to-kindle-workflow-2026/">Calibre EPUB-to-Kindle workflow</a> covers the full process, including how to <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">send EPUBs to Kindle</a> without a USB cable.</li>
  <li><strong>Library management:</strong> keep DRM-free EPUBs organized in Calibre so they stay searchable and re-convertible. Our <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre library management guide</a> shows how to keep metadata, covers, and formats consistent across a growing collection.</li>
</ol>

<p>The takeaway: indie storefront ebooks are most convenient on EPUB-native hardware, and perfectly usable on Kindles with a small conversion step. If you are comparing reading apps in the meantime, our <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">best EPUB reader apps guide</a> covers the software side of the ecosystem.</p>

<hr />

<h2 id="what-it-means">What This Means for You</h2>

<p>The Libro.fm ebook expansion is good news for readers, but it is worth weighing the trade-offs honestly:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>For indie storefront ebooks</th>
      <th>Things to keep in mind</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Support local bookstores</strong> with every purchase — the same indie model that made Libro.fm's audiobooks popular</td>
      <td><strong>App-first launch:</strong> no e-reader integration has been announced yet, so early ebooks will be read in the Libro.fm app</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>EPUB-based files</strong> that work across devices and apps, unlike Amazon's proprietary formats</td>
      <td><strong>DRM still applies</strong> to most storefront ebooks; Adobe DRM is the industry norm outside Amazon</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Flexible credits</strong> that can buy either format, plus LibroSync for hybrid reading</td>
      <td><strong>Catalog will be smaller</strong> than Amazon or Kobo at launch, at least initially</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Two credible indie options</strong> (Libro.fm + Bookshop.org) means competition and better features</td>
      <td><strong>Ecosystem is still maturing</strong> — Bookshop.org's Kobo integration was delayed for over a year</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>If you are already a Libro.fm audiobook subscriber, the ebook expansion is a natural reason to consolidate your purchases in one place. If you are a Kindle user who has been thinking about the <a href="/blog/why-authors-leaving-amazon-2026/">broader exodus from the Amazon ecosystem</a>, this is one more sign that the "buy once, read anywhere" future is getting closer — and EPUB is the format that makes it possible.</p>

<p>The trend that matters most: <strong>independent storefronts are finally taking ebooks seriously.</strong> Between Bookshop.org's 2025 launch, its upcoming Kobo integration, and now Libro.fm's expansion, readers who want to support local bookstores no longer have to choose between that value and digital convenience. For conversion-minded readers, the practical implication is straightforward: EPUB remains the common language of every non-Amazon store, which is exactly why knowing how to <a href="/blog/epub-vs-pdf-vs-mobi-vs-azw3/">manage EPUB and its format rivals</a> pays off.</p>

